    callout: "Floor-rated. The one large study on the question found that spending more on the ring and the wedding was associated with higher, not lower, hazard of divorce, while a large guest list and a honeymoon were associated with lower hazard. Financial strain and debt are themselves divorce predictors. Spend on the people and the trip, not the price tag.",
    description:
      "Using a survey of more than 3,000 ever-married US adults, economists found that relatively high spending on the engagement ring and the wedding ceremony was associated with a higher hazard of divorce, while higher wedding attendance and taking a honeymoon were associated with lower hazard. The study is observational — couples who overspend may differ in other ways — but the direction is the opposite of the industry claim, and separate work links financial problems and debt to divorce. Wedding rituals do matter as reinforcers of the role transition, which the attendance finding also suggests. The honest verdict: the ceremony's job is to gather your community, not to demonstrate expenditure.",
